mplementary visual zone. Do not generate navigation arrows, text bands, cards, or panels; the application adds functional overlays later.\n\nVISUAL EXECUTION: Retain the evidence-backed canvas brightness, palette placement, continuous background construction, illustration or photographic medium, shadow language, and recurring family rhythm. Evidence-backed product UI may remain inside the hero subject; never reproduce a band, card, or panel as a holder for renderer copy. The cta page must remain recognizably related to the other five pages while its subject hierarchy stays unique.\n\nDENSITY: Use one focal subject grouping plus at most two small evidence-backed supporting objects. Maintain a clear foreground, restrained midground, quiet continuous background, and immediate three-second comprehension.\n\nRESTRICTIONS: 1. Do not promote a minor reference icon into the primary hero. 2. Do not invent claims, metrics, people, photography, UI, or product capabilities. 3. Do not replace the measured family background with generic neon cybersecurity art. 4. Do not move generated visuals into the deterministic text-safe zone. 5. Do not render headline, paragraph, CTA, logo, watermark, brand name, or decorative fake text. 6. Do not change the observed illustration medium, depth, subject construction, or palette placement. Preserve real product UI only when it is the evidence-backed hero subject. 7. Do not reuse the same visual scene on another role; each page keeps its own measured anatomy. 8. Do not break the shared outer margin, navigation anchor, or recurring family rhythm. 9. Do not draw blank cards, empty panels, placeholder rectangles, statistic boxes, text-holder banners, fake forms, or framed copy areas. 10. A real evidence-backed product UI may be the hero subject in the visual zone, but it may not be simplified into an empty box for later text.\n\nFINAL OUTPUT: One continuous full-bleed carousel background with a deliberately empty renderer-aligned copy reserve, exact reference-derived page anatomy, no rendered marketing copy, and no logo.\n\nTechnical production contract: Renderer mapping: the right 54% is the text-safe zone and the left 46% is the visual zone; this mapping overrides any conflicting directional language above. Create negative space through subject placement, crop, lighting, blur, or a continuous color field: no text-holder box, no blank card, no placeholder panel, no empty banner, no framed copy area, and no callout rectangle. A real product screen or dashboard is allowed only as the primary evidence-backed subject in the visual zone, never as an empty copy container. No headline, no paragraph, no CTA text, no logo, no watermark, no brand name, no decorative fake text, no illegible UI labels, and no clipped primary subject.","slideIndex":6,"puppeteerConfig":{"cta":{"x":760,"y":900,"text":"Learn more","align":"center","bgColor":"#4A91E8","fontSize":22,"paddingX":28,"paddingY":14,"textColor":"#111216","borderRadius":12},"logoX":930,"logoY":48,"textX":null,"textY":null,"bgColor":"#102F49","padding":60,"ctaStyle":{"x":760,"y":900,"bgColor":"#4A91E8","fontSize":22,"paddingX":28,"paddingY":14,"textColor":"#111216","borderRadius":12},"elements":[],"logoSize":48,"removeBg":false,"textAlign":"left","uppercase":false,"accentSize":64,"bulletSize":19,"eyebrowGap":16,"fontFamily":"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif","splitRatio":46,"subheadGap":32,"accentColor":"#4A91E8","ctaPosition":"bottom-right","eyebrowSize":14,"headlineGap":22,"splitLayout":"left-image","eyebrowColor":"#0875F5","headlineSize":68,"logoPosition":"top-right","splitPercent":54,"textPosition":{"x":557,"y":480},"accentBgColor":null,"headlineColor":"#F3F8FD","microcopySize":24,"verticalAlign":"bottom","accentBgRadius":0,"accentPosition":"bottom-left","microcopyColor":"#F3F8FD","patternOpacity":0.12,"accentBgEnabled":false,"accentBgPadding":0,"accentUnderline":false,"textAccentColor":"#111216","eyebrowUppercase":false,"headlineFontFamily":"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif","microcopyFontFamily":"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif","accentUnderlineColor":"#0875F5"}}]}],"brandDescription":"## Brand summary\nAgencyAnalytics is an AI-powered marketing analytics and client-reporting platform purpose-built for marketing agencies. It brings client data from more than 85 sources into one place, then helps agency teams analyze performance, surface meaningful trends, and deliver polished reports and dashboards without spreadsheet-heavy or conventional BI workflows. The audience includes agency owners, account managers, strategists, analysts, client-service teams, and the clients who consume their reporting.\n\nThe central problem is reporting chaos: fragmented channel data, repetitive manual production, unclear performance narratives, and too little time for strategy. AgencyAnalytics addresses that problem with automated reports, customizable dashboards, reusable templates, drag-and-drop editing, AI-assisted analysis, alerts, anomaly detection, forecasting, benchmarks, client portals, white labeling, task management, and access controls. Its strongest differentiation is the combination of agency-specific workflows, multi-client visibility, client-ready presentation, broad data connectivity, and analysis that turns raw metrics into useful next steps.\n\nThe brand should consistently connect operational efficiency to a client outcome: agencies save time, explain results clearly, prove ROI, and make reporting a reason clients stay. Verified proof includes use by 7,000+ marketing agencies, 85+ data sources or integrations, and a reported 4.7/5 rating across G2 and Capterra based on 500+ reviews. The self-service offer includes a free 14-day trial with no credit card required.\n\nDerived direction: Carry forward the idea of AgencyAnalytics as the agency-specific operating layer that turns scattered client data into clear, branded client intelligence at scale. Every campaign should make the path from data to insight to client confidence easy to understand.\n\n## Visual identity\nThe verified asset set includes an AgencyAnalytics combination logo and large and small wordmark assets. The recognizable horizontal lockup combines an angular symbol with the single-line AgencyAnalytics wordmark; observed executions distinguish the two parts of the name through weight, with Agency lighter and Analytics heavier. Official and campaign examples use both blue and black presentations, so production teams should select an existing supplied asset rather than reconstructing the mark or approximating the wordmark.\n\nThe visual system is recognizable through a concentrated set of cues:\n\n- Bright blue paired with dark navy or near-black, white, and pale blue surfaces.\n- Clean, rounded interface cards with restrained shadows and visible data visualizations.\n- Product panels arranged as modular layers rather than hidden inside decorative scenery.\n- Upward movement expressed through the angular mark, charts, progress lines, goals, and performance indicators.\n- Pale grids, dotted accents, circular outlines, arcs, diagonal geometry, and blue-tinted lower fields in campaign compositions.\n- A clear split between concise campaign copy and a substantial product proof area.\n\nDerived direction: The visual personality should feel capable, clear, optimistic, and operationally calm. Make complex data look manageable without making the brand simplistic. For square campaign work, anchor the logo and copy in the upper portion and let a large product panel or layered UI cluster dominate the lower half. Use abstraction to frame the information, not to compete with it.\n\nNo verified clear-space ratio, minimum logo size, alternate-mark taxonomy, or corporate-logo background specification is supplied. Do not create numerical logo rules from preview guides or apply customer white-label upload requirements to the AgencyAnalytics corporate identity.\n\n## Color palette\nNo authoritative guideline color table was supplied. The following exact values come from measured campaign references and should be treated as the working digital palette for matching those executions, not as print or Pantone specifications:\n\n- **#0D80F4 ÔÇö primary bright blue:** Brand emphasis, highlighted words, active interface states, links, primary controls, and dominant chart lines.\n- **#102F49 ÔÇö dark navy:** Headings, high-emphasis supporting copy, dark buttons, and strong UI labels where a softer alternative to black is useful.\n- **#4A91E8 ÔÇö supporting blue:** Secondary chart emphasis, lighter interface accents, and tonal depth within blue compositions.\n- **#111216 ÔÇö near-black:** Primary text, black logo treatments, dark highlight bands, and maximum-emphasis campaign elements.\n- **#F3F8FD ÔÇö pale blue:** Section fields, soft transitions, low-emphasis panels, and spacious background areas.\n- **#FFFFFF ÔÇö white:** Main content surfaces, interface cards, reversed copy, and open negative space.\n\nObserved product visualizations add categorical colors such as cyan, green, lime, yellow, orange, pink, red, teal, lavender, and muted tan. These colors distinguish channels, segments, ranking bands, or status; they do not replace bright blue as the main brand and interaction color. Green is repeatedly associated with positive movement or enabled settings, while red can indicate negative or lower-status conditions. Client-specific white-label palettes are demonstrations of product flexibility and must not be mistaken for the AgencyAnalytics corporate palette.\n\nDerived direction: Build hierarchy with white or pale blue as the field, near-black or navy for primary reading, and #0D80F4 for the single most important phrase, control, or data signal. Keep secondary chart colors functionally distinct and restrained. No formal accessibility pairings were supplied, so validate contrast in the final medium rather than assuming that an observed pairing is compliant; use high-contrast navy or near-black for essential text and reserve light blue-on-white combinations for nonessential decoration.\n\n## Typography\nNo authoritative corporate typeface or licensed font family is identified in the supplied guideline material. Measured campaign references use an `Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif` implementation stack, with approximately 700 weight for headings and 400 for body copy. Treat this as a reproducible reference for the supplied creative, not as confirmation of a proprietary brand font.\n\nObserved hierarchy is consistent even where the exact family is unavailable:\n\n- Large headlines use a bold sans serif, short line lengths, and direct outcome-led phrasing.\n- Body copy uses a regular, highly legible sans serif with enough spacing to keep operational language approachable.\n- Product titles and field labels use clear weight changes rather than decorative styling.\n- KPI numerals are large and bold; descriptors, legends, and table labels are smaller and quieter.\n- Small uppercase eyebrow labels may introduce a category or workflow, while primary headlines and body copy remain sentence case.\n- Campaign emphasis may color one key word bright blue or place the latter phrase in a dark rounded block with white type.\n\nDerived direction: Typography should sound like an experienced agency operator: decisive in the headline, plainspoken in the explanation, and precise in the data. Use weight, color, and compact phrase grouping to establish hierarchy. Avoid expressive display lettering, excessive all-caps copy, or dense paragraphs that make the platform feel more complicated than the workflow it replaces.\n\n## Style\nThe official messaging pattern is direct, practical, second-person, and agency-literate. It names a familiar operational problem, explains the workflow, and closes on a business outcome. Recurring concepts include `client-ready`, `prove ROI`, `save time`, `one place`, `without the manual work`, `white label`, `insights`, `client intelligence`, and `reporting chaos`. Prefer concrete verbs such as connect, automate, track, surface, share, monitor, compare, customize, and scale. Explain results in plain English rather than adding BI jargon.\n\n## CTA\nThe primary self-service CTA language is `Start Free`, `Start Free Trial`, or, in the closing conversion section, `Start Your Free Trial Today`. Pair trial conversion with the verified reassurance `Free 14-day trial. No credit card required.` The assisted-sales alternative is `Book Demo` or `Book a Demo`.\n\nThe recurring action hierarchy is:\n\n- **Primary conversion:** `Start Free` or `Start Free Trial` for users ready to enter the product.\n- **Secondary conversion:** `Book Demo` or `Book a Demo` for buyers who want a guided, agency-specific walkthrough.\n- **Product education:** `Watch the Platform Tour`.\n- **Workflow discovery:** `Start With a Template` and `Find Your Integrations`.\n- **Solution progression:** `Speed Up Client Onboarding`, `Strengthen Client Relationships`, and `Maintain Complete Client Visibility`.\n\nPrimary and secondary conversion actions appear together immediately after high-level value propositions; trial language is also repeated near the end of the page. Supporting CTAs sit beside the workflow or resource they describe. Action language is concise, imperative, and specific rather than abstract.\n\nDerived direction: Preserve the two-track conversion model in campaign work: immediate product access first, human guidance second. Use one primary CTA and at most one assisted alternative in a single static unit. Do not invent discounts, guarantees, urgency, or trial terms beyond the verified 14-day and no-credit-card statements.\n\n## Background imagery\nEvidenced imagery includes bright workplace photography, attributed customer headshots, team collaboration, and product-focused compositions. People are shown discussing work, using laptops, or participating in agency activity rather than posing as generic technology users. Lighting is natural and bright, environments are warm and neutral, and the overall treatment remains realistic and full color.\n\nProduct-led backgrounds use white or pale blue fields transitioning into more saturated cyan or royal blue, often in the lower third. Repeated decorative ingredients include faint square grids, sparse dots, oversized circular outlines, thin arcs, diagonal shapes, and soft tonal glows. Other campaign references use flat pale pink or peach fields behind report and dashboard mockups. A saturated blue report-cover treatment with an oversized cropped angular or swooping form is also evidenced.\n\nDerived direction: Choose a background according to the proof being shown. Use a clean pale field for dense UI, a blue gradient field for broad platform or intelligence messaging, and workplace photography when the idea depends on agency collaboration or time returned to people. Preserve an uncluttered area for the headline and CTA. Crop photography so faces and working gestures remain clear while the less active side of the frame accepts UI overlays.\n\nDerived direction: Avoid busy stock-office scenes, dark cinematic technology imagery, heavy textures, fake data tunnels, or scenic lifestyle backgrounds with no relationship to agency work. Do not place essential copy over the most detailed part of a photograph or dashboard.\n\n## UI mockup\nFor the central campaign messageÔÇöturning client data into client intelligenceÔÇöthe most relevant verified workflow is a unified client dashboard paired with AI analysis that surfaces trends, dips, opportunities, summaries, or recommended next steps. This shows both halves of the promise: connected performance data and the interpretation that makes it actionable. If a campaign instead focuses on automation, use report scheduling; for branding, use a white-labeled dashboard or report; for proactive monitoring, use anomaly annotations or metric alerts; for retention, use the client portal; and for operational visibility, use a multi-client dashboard, roll-up view, goals, or tasks.\n\nObserved marketing treatments show the interface directly as a front-facing application window or cropped panel rather than inside a laptop or phone frame. Screens may include real reports, dashboards, KPI cards, charts, tables, the drag-and-drop builder, AI Summary, Ask AI, scheduling controls, client overview, goals, anomaly detection, access management, or task management. Use only approved product captures and existing interface states; do not fabricate navigation, client names, metrics, trends, alerts, AI answers, or integrations.\n\nDerived direction: For the default intelligence campaign, place one large front-facing dashboard in the lower or right portion of the composition and pull one verified AI insight or analysis panel forward as the focal interaction. Keep perspective minimal so labels and visualizations remain credible. Show a small number of KPI cards and one principal chart or table rather than an unreadable wall of widgets. Leave the upper-left or opposite side clear for headline, support copy, and CTA; no essential text should overlap the UI.\n\nDerived direction: Device framing is unnecessary unless an approved source asset already uses it or mobile access is itself the message. Human-plus-product scenes are appropriate when the campaign is about collaboration, client conversations, or time saved; otherwise, direct product evidence is stronger. When no verified screen directly supports the claim, use no UI rather than inventing one.\n\n## Icons and illustration\nThe evidenced icon role is functional and informational. Product controls use compact symbols for reports, dashboards, charts, alerts, integrations, users, time, close, add, dropdown, and AI or sparkle actions. They are generally simple, low-detail, and monochrome in dark navy or gray, with blue used for active, selected, or branded actions. An extracted analytics integration asset uses three colored bars, while third-party integration logos retain their recognizable forms and original colors.\n\nStandalone illustration is not the dominant system. Marketing work relies more on direct product screenshots, photography-plus-UI collage, charts, geometric fields, and the angular directional geometry associated with the brand mark. Circular avatars, initials, and profile photos may support collaboration or access-management scenes.\n\nDerived direction: Use icons to clarify controls, categories, or stepsÔÇönot as the main visual spectacle. Keep forms compact and geometric, maintain consistent apparent stroke weight within a single set, and avoid mixing detailed pictograms with minimal interface symbols. Preserve original integration logos when naming real connected platforms rather than redrawing them into a house style.\n\nDerived direction: If illustration is required, keep it geometric, data-oriented, and subordinate to product evidence. Suitable ingredients are charts, progress paths, modular cards, directional arrows, grids, and cropped angular forms. Do not introduce mascots, hand-drawn editorial characters, elaborate isometric worlds, or unrelated 3D iconography; no such system is evidenced.","visualBlueprints":{"staticSystem":{"zones":["AgencyAnalytics logo lockup (logo): x=7%, y=5%, width=26%, height=4%; foreground; Black circular angular emblem followed by the black wordmark; the second word is heavier than the first","Reports Clients Love headline (headline): x=8%, y=14%, width=34%, height=4%; foreground; Single-line bold sans-serif headline in black, with the middle word Clients highlighted in bright blue","Supporting statement (other): x=8%, y=21%, width=50%, height=6%; foreground; Two lines of heavy black sans-serif text, left aligned, with a wide line break after the words save time","Learn More button (other): x=7%, y=34%, width=20%, height=6%; foreground; Dark navy horizontal pill with centered bold white label","Faint grid and dot field (pattern): x=0%, y=15%, width=100%, height=61%; background; Thin low-contrast white grid lines concentrated across the right and lower fields, plus small dotted matrices near the upper-right and left-middle edges","Analytics application frame (illustration): x=10%, y=40%, width=59%, height=43%; midground; Large white rounded software window with a dark vertical navigation rail, pale top toolbar, light gray card borders, and soft shadow; its lower-right area is obscured","Main line-chart card (card): x=16%, y=47%, width=29%, height=11%; midground; Wide white rounded card with pale horizontal guides, gray label strokes, and a thin blue rising line graph","Donut-chart card (KPI): x=46%, y=47%, width=20%, height=11%; midground; White rounded card containing a blue and pale-gray donut chart, four blue circular legend marks, and gray text strokes","Three compact chart cards (card): x=16%, y=59%, width=50%, height=9%; midground; Row of three white rounded modules: a blue line chart, an orange line chart, and a seven-column blue bar chart","Lower dashboard modules (card): x=16%, y=70%, width=50%, height=11%; midground; Two broad white rounded cards with blue bullet marks, gray text strokes, and short blue and orange progress lines; center and right portions are covered by the laptop and person","Floating status tiles (KPI): x=3%, y=73%, width=27%, height=19%; foreground; Three overlapping translucent blue rounded squares with thin white borders and glow; icons depict a clock, checkmark, and three ascending bars","Dotted curved connector (arrow): x=23%, y=79%, width=14%, height=6%; foreground; Short white dotted curve descending toward the right between the floating tiles and central foreground","Seated woman (other): x=56%, y=33%, width=45%, height=62%; foreground; Photographic three-quarter view of a dark-haired woman in a blue button-up shirt, looking downward while typing; cropped by the right edge and lower desk area","Open laptop (other): x=31%, y=72%, width=48%, height=26%; foreground; Open silver laptop in diagonal perspective; the back of the screen faces the viewer and overlaps the dashboard and the woman''s torso and hands","Desk notebook and pen (other): x=72%, y=92%, width=28%, height=8%; foreground; Open cream-colored notebook cropped by the bottom and right edges, with a dark pen lying diagonally near the upper-right page","Potted plant (other): x=0%, y=84%, width=11%, height=17%; foreground; Small green spiky plant in a dark pot, partially cropped by the left and bottom canvas edges","Brand zone: x 7.4, y 4.7, width 26.3, height 4.1 percent","Headline zone: x 7.5, y 14.1, width 33.7, height 3.8 percent","Supporting-copy zone: x 7.7, y 21.1, width 49.5, height 6.2 percent","Call-to-action zone: x 7.4, y 34.3, width 20.3, height 5.8 percent","Dashboard zone: x 9.5, y 39.6, width 59.1, height 42.7 percent","Person zone: x 55.5, y 32.8, width 44.5, height 61.7 percent","Laptop zone: x 31.4, y 71.5, width 47.8, height 26.3 percent","Floating-tile zone: x 3.2, y 73.2, width 26.8, height 19.2 percent"],"canvas":["Canvas: square, aspect 1:1, White upper field transitioning through very pale blue into saturated cyan-blue across the lower third, with faint white grid lines and dotted accents over the blue-tinted area","Dominant visible colors: #FFFFFF, #F3F8FD, #0D80F4, #102F49, #111216, #4A91E8","Outer margin is approximately 7% of canvas width","Bright square canvas with a white upper base and blue lower transition","No visible outer border or corner rounding","Primary left margin is approximately 7.5 percent","Right-side photography and bottom objects run into the canvas edges","Open whitespace is concentrated above the dashboard and around the upper text"],"invariants":["Preserve the square canvas.","Keep the brand and promotional copy aligned to the same upper-left margin.","Keep the headline above the supporting statement and button.","Retain a single blue word within the black headline.","Maintain the dashboard as a large middle-plane rectangle beginning near 40 percent canvas height.","Keep the photographic subject dominant on the right.","Keep the laptop crossing the lower center in front of the dashboard.","Maintain a white upper field and saturated blue lower field.","Preserve cropped foreground objects along the bottom edge.","Keep the button as a dark horizontal pill."],"imageryAndDepth":["The dominant photographic subject spans x 55.5 to 100 and y 32.8 to 94.5 percent.","The software window occupies the middle plane behind the person and laptop.","The laptop sits in front of both the dashboard and the person''s torso.","The person''s arms and hands overlap the laptop keyboard area.","Floating glass-like tiles sit in front of the blue background and dashboard edge.","The plant, laptop base, notebook, and pen are cropped by the bottom boundary.","Lighting is bright and soft, with the photographed subject illuminated from the front-left.","Subtle shadows under the application frame, tiles, and laptop establish depth."],"prohibitedDrift":["Do not convert the composition into a symmetric two-column split.","Do not center the logo or headline.","Do not move the photographed subject into the upper-left text field.","Do not enlarge the floating icons into the dominant visual.","Do not replace the dashboard with a phone, tablet, or abstract panel.","Do not turn the background into a dark field.","Do not add a border, frame, or rounded canvas edge.","Do not replace the photographed subject with multiple people.","Do not introduce large numerical KPI typography.","Do not remove the layered occlusion between dashboard, laptop, and person."],"colorAndContrast":["Black logo and copy sit on white for maximum contrast.","Bright blue is confined to the headline highlight, interface charts, selected controls, icon tiles, and lower background.","The call-to-action uses white text on dark navy.","Dashboard cards use white surfaces with pale gray borders and labels.","The blue shirt echoes the interface and background blues.","Orange appears only as a small secondary chart accent.","The lower blue field contrasts with the white dashboard and pale foreground tiles."],"densityAndRhythm":["Four primary promotional blocks appear before the visual content: logo, headline, supporting copy, and button.","The dashboard contains one large line-chart card, one donut card, three compact middle cards, and two broad lower modules.","Three floating status tiles overlap in a descending-right sequence.","Reading order proceeds from logo to headline, supporting statement, button, dashboard, then person and foreground objects.","The upper-right area remains mostly open except for faint patterning and the person''s head.","The lower half is visually dense due to overlapping interface, person, laptop, tiles, plant, and notebook.","Vertical gaps are largest between the supporting statement and button and between the logo and headline.","Most module gaps inside the dashboard are narrow and regular."],"referenceIndexes":[1],"typographyBehavior":["All visible promotional copy is left aligned.","The headline is a single bold line with tight spacing.","One interior headline word receives blue color emphasis without a separate box.","The supporting statement is smaller than the headline but remains heavy and high contrast.","The supporting copy uses two lines of similar length.","The button label is centered, bold, and white.","The logo wordmark combines lighter and heavier weights.","Dashboard text is represented mostly by short pale-gray strokes rather than legible long copy."],"recurringComponents":["Black logo emblem and wordmark lockup","Left-aligned headline with one blue highlighted word","Two-line supporting statement","Dark navy rounded action button","Rounded white application frame","Dark vertical application navigation rail","White bordered chart cards","Blue line, donut, and bar graphics","Translucent blue status tiles with white outline icons","Low-contrast grid and dotted background marks"]},"carouselSystem":{"zones":["Pale cyan gradient canvas (other): x=0%, y=0%, width=100%, height=100%; background; Borderless square background with pale cyan edges, a diffuse near-white central area, and no visible texture or pattern.","Blue eyebrow label (headline): x=6%, y=15%, width=17%, height=1%; foreground; Small single-line blue sans-serif text reading ''Proactive client reporting''; left aligned and moderately bold.","Primary headline (headline): x=6%, y=17%, width=37%, height=7%; foreground; Large bold black sans-serif headline reading ''The next steps''; ''next steps'' is reversed in white inside a black rounded rectangle.","Headline highlight block (band): x=16%, y=17%, width=27%, height=7%; midground; Solid black horizontal rounded rectangle behind the white words ''next steps'', with approximately 1% corner radius.","Supporting statement (headline): x=6%, y=26%, width=35%, height=2%; foreground; Single line of dark charcoal bold sans-serif text reading ''Implement proactive measures today''.","First action bullet (list): x=6%, y=31%, width=39%, height=2%; foreground; Small bright-blue circular bullet followed by bold black text reading ''Utilize automated reporting tools''.","Second action bullet (list): x=6%, y=36%, width=40%, height=2%; foreground; Small bright-blue circular bullet followed by bold black text reading ''Schedule regular client check-ins''.","Desktop schedule dashboard (card): x=22%, y=51%, width=51%, height=37%; midground; Large white rounded application panel with a faint gray border and soft shadow. It contains a narrow icon sidebar, a ''Schedule'' header, blue and gray tabs, a blue report button, three bordered schedule rows, and a four-column metrics strip.","Dashboard navigation rail (other): x=22%, y=51%, width=5%, height=37%; foreground; White vertical sidebar separated by a thin gray divider; six stacked gray or blue navigation icons, with the top grid icon inside a pale-blue rounded square.","Dashboard schedule list (list): x=30%, y=57%, width=41%, height=18%; foreground; Three white horizontal report rows within a faint rounded border. Each row has a pale-blue icon tile, two lines of dark and gray text, a green status dot with label, and a vertical overflow mark.","Dashboard KPI strip (KPI): x=30%, y=76%, width=41%, height=9%; foreground; White rounded metrics module divided into four vertical columns labeled Reports Sent, Automation Rate, Time Saved, and Clients Updated; each has a bold value and a small blue rising sparkline.","Mobile reminders screen (card): x=6%, y=66%, width=17%, height=28%; midground; Tall white smartphone with heavily rounded top corners, thin gray edge, status bar, Reminders heading, bell icon, and stacked pale report cards; lower portion is cropped and overlapped by the identity badge.","Floating calendar card (card): x=72%, y=57%, width=22%, height=25%; foreground; White rounded rectangle with a soft shadow. It shows a centered ''May 2025'' heading, left and right chevrons, weekday initials, a seven-column date grid, and four dates inside solid blue circles.","Agency analytics identity badge (logo): x=6%, y=90%, width=18%, height=5%; foreground; Black pill-shaped badge overlapping the phone. A white circular area at left contains a black-and-white angular emblem, followed by white ''@agencyanalytics'' text.","Background zone: x=0%, y=0%, width=100%, height=100%.","Eyebrow zone: x=5.5%, y=15.1%, width=17.2%, height=1.4%.","Headline zone: x=5.5%, y=17.3%, width=36.6%, height=6.5%.","Supporting-copy zone: x=5.5%, y=25.7%, width=34.5%, height=2.3%.","Action-list zone: x=5.5%, y=31.4%, width=39.7%, height=7.2%.","Open-space zone: x=47%, y=12%, width=47%, height=37%.","Desktop dashboard zone: x=22.1%, y=50.6%, width=51%, height=37.4%.","Phone zone: x=6.4%, y=65.8%, width=16.7%, height=28.2%.","Calendar zone: x=71.9%, y=57.1%, width=22.3%, height=25.1%.","Identity badge zone: x=5.5%, y=90%, width=17.8%, height=4.6%."],"canvas":["Canvas: square, aspect 1:1, Full-bleed pale cyan field with a soft white glow through the center an