erved crop, overlap, whitespace rhythm, and edge alignment. The reserve stays visually continuous with the canvas and has no border, fill patch, card, or panel.\n\nVISUAL EXECUTION: Use saved palette roles #0D80F4, #102F49, and #4A91E8 only where the evidence supports them. Reproduce the observed flat-versus-dimensional balance, corner radii, border weight, shadows, illustration geometry, and foreground/midground/background order.\n\nDENSITY AND LEGIBILITY: One compact corner subject and the largest negative-space reserve of the five layouts; no callout shapes or text placeholders. Retain one decisive focal point and enough continuous negative space to understand the visual in three seconds. Tiny functional marks may be abstract shapes only; they must not resemble generated marketing text or empty UI.\n\nRESTRICTIONS: 1. Do not replace the uploaded composition with a generic topic symbol or cinematic wallpaper. 2. Do not promote a minor reference icon into the primary hero. 3. Do not put visual modules, texture, glow, or illustrations inside the renderer text-safe zone. 4. Do not invent claims, customer metrics, product capabilities, certifications, or interfaces. 5. Do not change a light reference into a dark neon canvas or a flat reference into photorealism. 6. Do not add people, devices, shields, locks, circuits, dashboards, or charts unless the reference supports them. 7. Do not increase the observed subject, icon, chart, or illustration count merely to fill space. An evidence-backed dashboard may remain one complete hero subject. 8. Do not imitate long source copy; reproduce only the visible design grammar and subject or scene anatomy. 9. Do not render headline, body copy, CTA, logo, watermark, brand name, or decorative fake text. 10. Do not crop the primary visual, break the outer margin, or cover the deterministic overlay reserve. 11. Do not draw a blank card, empty panel, placeholder rectangle, banner, text-holder box, callout box, or framed copy area; negative space must remain part of the continuous background. 12. Every visual-side module must be complete and meaningful: no blank white cards, empty rounded tiles, vacant UI rows, skeleton widgets, unused list panels, or containers waiting for copy. A module is allowed only when its interior is fully occupied by one coherent nonverbal illustration, icon, product view, or abstract unlabeled visualization; otherwise remove it. 13. A real product dashboard, report, device, or application screen may appear only as the evidence-backed hero subject in the visual zone, never as a container for overlay copy.\n\nFINAL OUTPUT: One continuous full-bleed, reference-faithful square background with a deliberately empty overlay region and one clearly art-directed visual systemÔÇöno rendered marketing copy and no logo.\n\nTechnical production contract: Renderer mapping: the left 58% is the text-safe zone and the right 42% is the visual zone; this mapping overrides any conflicting directional language above. Create negative space through subject placement, crop, lighting, blur, or a continuous color field: no text-holder box, no blank card, no placeholder panel, no empty banner, no framed copy area, and no callout rectangle. A real product screen or dashboard is allowed only as the primary evidence-backed subject in the visual zone, never as an empty copy container. No headline, no paragraph, no CTA text, no logo, no watermark, no brand name, no decorative fake text, no illegible UI labels, and no clipped primary subject.","puppeteerConfig":{"cta":{"x":60,"y":900,"text":"Learn more","align":"left","bgColor":"#4A91E8","fontSize":22,"paddingX":28,"paddingY":14,"textColor":"#111216","borderRadius":12},"logoX":60,"logoY":48,"textX":0,"textY":190,"bgColor":"#102F49","padding":60,"ctaStyle":{"x":60,"y":900,"bgColor":"#4A91E8","fontSize":22,"paddingX":28,"paddingY":14,"textColor":"#111216","borderRadius":12},"elements":[],"logoSize":48,"removeBg":false,"textAlign":"left","uppercase":false,"accentSize":64,"bulletSize":21,"eyebrowGap":18,"fontFamily":"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif","splitRatio":42,"subheadGap":32,"accentColor":"#FFFFFF","ctaPosition":"bottom-left","eyebrowSize":15,"headlineGap":24,"splitLayout":"right-image","eyebrowColor":"#FFFFFF","headlineSize":64,"logoPosition":"top-left","splitPercent":58,"textPosition":{"x":60,"y":250},"accentBgColor":null,"headlineColor":"#F3F8FD","microcopySize":25,"verticalAlign":"center","accentBgRadius":0,"accentPosition":"bottom-left","microcopyColor":"#F3F8FD","patternOpacity":0.12,"accentBgEnabled":false,"accentBgPadding":0,"accentUnderline":false,"textAccentColor":"#FFFFFF","eyebrowUppercase":false,"headlineFontFamily":"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif","microcopyFontFamily":"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif","accentUnderlineColor":"#FFFFFF"}}],"infographicLayouts":[{"name":"Demo Brand Hero Metric","isActive":true,"description":"PURPOSE: Create a single-insight 1080x1080 motion infographic in which one verified number, percentage, ratio, or short proof statement is the dominant object.\n\nCONTENT ROUTING: Use only when the supplied copy contains one clearly dominant metric or proof point. Route supporting facts into no more than two secondary callouts. If no numeric fact exists, use the strongest supplied short statement as a typographic hero without fabricating a number.\n\nCANVAS ZONES: Use 72px safe margins. Reserve the top 16% for logo and eyebrow, the next 22% for the headline, the central 38% for the hero metric and its visual encoding, and the bottom 18% for explanation or an explicit CTA. Keep at least 4% breathing room between zones.\n\nCOMPONENT ANATOMY: Construct one hero-number group, one directly labelled visual indicator, zero to two supporting callouts, and an optional CTA/end card. Cards use the radii, borders, padding, and motif rules from the saved guide.\n\nDATA/CHART BEHAVIOR: Prefer a count-up, progress ring, single horizontal bar, or one-category comparison. The visual must encode only the supplied value and must remain understandable without a legend.\n\nTYPOGRAPHY AND COLOR APPLICATION: Use Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif at weight 700 for headlines, hero numbers, and section anchors; use Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif at weight 400 for labels and explanations. Build the palette from primary #0D80F4, secondary #102F49, accent #4A91E8, dark #111216, light #F3F8FD, and text #111216. Select foreground/background pairs only when they remain legible; the saved infographic brand guide is authoritative when it defines a stricter pairing.\n\nOVERFLOW/FITTING RULES: Measure every line before drawing. Reduce font size in 2px steps only after trying a shorter line break; never drop below 52px for the headline, 72px for the hero metric, 24px for supporting copy, or 18px for labels. Limit headlines to three lines, labels to two lines, and body modules to four lines. If content still overflows, reduce module count or switch to the next compatible layout instead of clipping.\n\nRESTRICTIONS: Do not invent claims, statistics, categories, comparisons, steps, CTA text, or product capabilities. Do not use random colors, decorative charts, generic neon technology styling, dense microtext, simultaneous entrances, uncontrolled glow, or a logo drawn by AI. Keep every animated layer independently addressable for future motion export.\n\nMOTION SEQUENCE: Background settles from 0-12%; logo/eyebrow enters at 8-20%; headline reveals at 15-34%; hero metric counts from 28-62%; the chart draws from 38-70%; supporting callouts stagger by 80ms from 58-78%; CTA enters at 74-88%. Use cubic ease-out, hold the completed state for the final 12%, and replace all transforms with a single opacity transition under reduced motion.\n\nFINAL OUTPUT: One brand-specific, data-first composition that communicates the main insight within two seconds and finishes on a stable, exportable end frame."},{"name":"Demo Brand Modular Insight Grid","isActive":true,"description":"PURPOSE: Create a balanced multi-stat, multi-benefit, or capability infographic using a small repeatable module system.\n\nCONTENT ROUTING: Use for two to four parallel facts, benefits, risks, categories, or proof points of comparable importance. If one fact is materially stronger, promote it to a double-width lead module and keep the remaining modules secondary.\n\nCANVAS ZONES: Use 72px outer margins and a 24px gutter. Reserve 18% for the header, 64% for a 2x2 or lead-plus-support grid, and 14% for a footer insight or explicit CTA. Never exceed four information modules.\n\nCOMPONENT ANATOMY: Every module contains one icon or small data mark, one metric or short heading, and one concise explanation. Apply consistent padding and alignment while allowing a lead module to span two columns. Use direct labels rather than detached legends.\n\nDATA/CHART BEHAVIOR: Use mini bars, compact rings, ordinal markers, or text-led KPI cards. All modules share one scale logic; do not mix unrelated chart types merely for variety.\n\nTYPOGRAPHY AND COLOR APPLICATION: Use Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif at weight 700 for headlines, hero numbers, and section anchors; use Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif at weight 400 for labels and explanations. Build the palette from primary #0D80F4, secondary #102F49, accent #4A91E8, dark #111216, light #F3F8FD, and text #111216. Select foreground/background pairs only when they remain legible; the saved infographic brand guide is authoritative when it defines a stricter pairing.\n\nOVERFLOW/FITTING RULES: Measure every line before drawing. Reduce font size in 2px steps only after trying a shorter line break; never drop below 52px for the headline, 72px for the hero metric, 24px for supporting copy, or 18px for labels. Limit headlines to three lines, labels to two lines, and body modules to four lines. If content still overflows, reduce module count or switch to the next compatible layout instead of clipping.\n\nRESTRICTIONS: Do not invent claims, statistics, categories, comparisons, steps, CTA text, or product capabilities. Do not use random colors, decorative charts, generic neon technology styling, dense microtext, simultaneous entrances, uncontrolled glow, or a logo drawn by AI. Keep every animated layer independently addressable for future motion export.\n\nMOTION SEQUENCE: Reveal the header at 10-28%; draw the grid frame at 20-36%; introduce modules in reading order with 90ms stagger from 30-72%; count metrics or draw mini charts within each module after its card appears; reveal the footer at 72-88%. Hold the complete grid. Reduced motion uses sequential fades with no scale, bounce, or parallax.\n\nFINAL OUTPUT: One coherent modular infographic whose modules can be animated separately while retaining a stable grid and clear mobile-scale hierarchy."},{"name":"Demo Brand Process Flow","isActive":true,"description":"PURPOSE: Explain a supplied sequence, workflow, timeline, or cause-and-effect chain as an unmistakable ordered progression.\n\nCONTENT ROUTING: Use only when the copy provides two to five explicit steps, stages, dates, or transitions. Preserve the supplied order and wording. If the content is not sequential, route it to the modular or comparison layout.\n\nCANVAS ZONES: Reserve 18% for the header, 66% for the flow, and 12% for the conclusion or CTA inside 72px safe margins. Use a horizontal path for two or three short steps and a vertical or serpentine path for four or five steps. Maintain at least 28px between step modules.\n\nCOMPONENT ANATOMY: Each step contains an ordinal marker, short title, concise explanation, and optional evidence-backed icon. Connectors must indicate direction without crossing text. The final step may receive the accent treatment but must not imply an invented outcome.\n\nDATA/CHART BEHAVIOR: Use progress lines, milestone dots, or stage cards rather than quantitative charts unless the copy supplies a value for a specific stage. Never manufacture durations or completion percentages.\n\nTYPOGRAPHY AND COLOR APPLICATION: Use Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif at weight 700 for headlines, hero numbers, and section anchors; use Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif at weight 400 for labels and explanations. Build the palette from primary #0D80F4, secondary #102F49, accent #4A91E8, dark #111216, light #F3F8FD, and text #111216. Select foreground/background pairs only when they remain legible; the saved infographic brand guide is authoritative when it defines a stricter pairing.\n\nOVERFLOW/FITTING RULES: Measure every line before drawing. Reduce font size in 2px steps only after trying a shorter line break; never drop below 52px for the headline, 72px for the hero metric, 24px for supporting copy, or 18px for labels. Limit headlines to three lines, labels to two lines, and body modules to four lines. If content still overflows, reduce module count or switch to the next compatible layout instead of clipping.\n\nRESTRICTIONS: Do not invent claims, statistics, categories, comparisons, steps, CTA text, or product capabilities. Do not use random colors, decorative charts, generic neon technology styling, dense microtext, simultaneous entrances, uncontrolled glow, or a logo drawn by AI. Keep every animated layer independently addressable for future motion export.\n\nMOTION SEQUENCE: Reveal the headline at 10-28%; draw the connector progressively from 24-64%; introduce each milestone immediately after the line reaches it with 100ms stagger; animate supplied numbers locally; emphasize the final stage at 68-82%; reveal the conclusion at 76-90%. Reduced motion shows the complete connector and fades milestones in reading order.\n\nFINAL OUTPUT: One motion-ready process composition with explicit reading order, independently addressable steps, and a stable final state that preserves the supplied sequence."},{"name":"Demo Brand Comparison Story","isActive":true,"description":"PURPOSE: Present a supplied before/after, option-versus-option, problem/solution, or editorial contrast without biasing or inventing either side.\n\nCONTENT ROUTING: Use when the copy contains two explicit states, groups, periods, approaches, or outcomes. Use a 50/50 comparison for equal weight, 42/58 when one side is the stated resolution, and an editorial stacked story when the copy contains context followed by evidence and conclusion.\n\nCANVAS ZONES: Use 72px safe margins. Reserve 18% for the shared headline, 64% for two columns or two stacked bands separated by a 24px gutter, and 14% for the verified takeaway or explicit CTA. Keep corresponding elements aligned across both sides.\n\nCOMPONENT ANATOMY: Each side contains a label, one dominant fact or statement, up to three matched attributes, and an evidence-backed visual mark. Use shared row heights and direct labels. Apply accent emphasis only to the side the supplied copy explicitly prioritizes.\n\nDATA/CHART BEHAVIOR: Use matched bars, paired KPI cards, delta arrows only when a real change is stated, or a two-state diagram. Use identical quantitative scales across both sides and show missing data honestly.\n\nTYPOGRAPHY AND COLOR APPLICATION: Use Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif at weight 700 for headlines, hero numbers, and section anchors; use Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif at weight 400 for labels and explanations. Build the palette from primary #0D80F4, secondary #102F49, accent #4A91E8, dark #111216, light #F3F8FD, and text #111216. Select foreground/background pairs only when they remain legible; the saved infographic brand guide is authoritative when it defines a stricter pairing.\n\nOVERFLOW/FITTING RULES: Measure every line before drawing. Reduce font size in 2px steps only after trying a shorter line break; never drop below 52px for the headline, 72px for the hero metric, 24px for supporting copy, or 18px for labels. Limit headlines to three lines, labels to two lines, and body modules to four lines. If content still overflows, reduce module count or switch to the next compatible layout instead of clipping.\n\nRESTRICTIONS: Do not invent claims, statistics, categories, comparisons, steps, CTA text, or product capabilities. Do not use random colors, decorative charts, generic neon technology styling, dense microtext, simultaneous entrances, uncontrolled glow, or a logo drawn by AI. Keep every animated layer independently addressable for future motion export.\n\nMOTION SEQUENCE: Reveal the common headline at 8-24%; introduce the baseline/divider at 20-34%; reveal the first state at 28-52% and the second at 42-68%; draw matched data marks together from 52-74%; emphasize only the supplied difference at 70-84%; reveal the takeaway at 78-92%. Reduced motion uses paired opacity fades and no sliding sides.\n\nFINAL OUTPUT: One comparison or editorial composition with fair scales, aligned evidence, clear brand expression, and no unsupported superiority claim."}],"infographicGuidelines":"## Evidence index\nPalette evidence: white, pale cyan, bright blue, navy, black, with occasional orange chart accents. Type evidence: heavy geometric sans-serif headlines, compact sans-serif support copy, and selective blue or white-on-black emphasis; exact fonts are unverified. Motifs: faint grids, dots, arcs, blue waves, pill CTAs, rounded analytics cards. Imagery: layered dashboards, laptops, cropped working subjects. Layouts: open upper copy fields over dense lower product scenes."}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, '2026-08-08T13:01:34.030175+00:00', '2026-08-08T13:21:40.240301+00:00', '2026-08-08T13:21:39.442+00:00') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
SET session_replication_role = DEFAULT;
COMMIT;
